# 7. REAL 3D AND SCROLL-DRIVEN ANIMATION REQUIREMENTS

The 3D experience must be technically real, not an imitation built from a static image.

First, inspect the existing project and select the most appropriate implementation approach.

For a React project, consider a combination of:

* Three.js.
* React Three Fiber.
* Drei, if useful.
* GSAP ScrollTrigger.
* Framer Motion or Motion for appropriate interface transitions.

Do not install every library automatically.

Use the smallest reliable set of tools that supports the requested experience.

If the project already includes a suitable animation or 3D framework, prefer reusing it.

### 7.1 Actual spatial depth

Use genuine visual depth, which may include:

* Perspective cameras.
* Three-dimensional card placement.
* Image textures.
* Controlled rotations.
* Layered geometry.
* Lighting and shadows.
* Z-axis motion.
* Scroll-linked position changes.
* Spatial transitions between story scenes.

A carefully implemented CSS 3D composition is acceptable for smaller components, but the overall experience must have convincing depth and controlled motion.

Do not claim to have implemented a Three.js scene if the implementation is only a flat CSS layout.

### 7.2 Scroll-driven storytelling

The animation must respond to the user's actual scroll position.

Examples:

* Cards move forward as the visitor approaches a section.
* Secondary cards shift backward.
* Event imagery transitions between story scenes.
* The active scene gains visual prominence.
* Budget components assemble into a readable chart.
* Theme cards reorganize into a gallery.
* The vendor section transforms into a comparison composition.
* The final CTA appears after the product story.

Use smooth progress-based transitions rather than excessive animation triggers.

### 7.3 Scrolling behaviour

Native vertical scrolling must continue to work naturally.

Do not:

* Lock the user into a section unnecessarily.
* Intercept the mouse wheel without a compelling reason.
* Force horizontal scrolling.
* Break the browser's scroll position.
* Make navigation impossible when animations are active.

Sticky storytelling sections can be used where appropriate, but they must release naturally at the end of the section.

### 7.4 Animation quality

Use:

* Smooth easing.
* Appropriate stagger.
* Controlled motion.
* Subtle perspective changes.
* Consistent durations.
* Meaningful transitions.
* Smooth entry and exit.
* Stable layouts.

Avoid:

* Unnecessary bouncing.
* Flashing.
* Excessive zoom.
* Constant random rotation.
* Animation that interferes with text.
* Large, uncontrolled particle effects.

### 7.5 Performance

The landing page must remain usable on ordinary laptops and mobile phones.

Use:

* Appropriately sized images.
* Lazy loading for lower-page imagery.
* Efficient 3D scene rendering.
* Reusable geometry and materials where suitable.
* GPU-friendly transformations.
* Cleanup of event listeners and animation instances.
* Proper disposal of graphics resources.
* Reasonable limits on continuous animation.
* Reduced complexity on lower-powered devices.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

If WebGL is unavailable or unsuitable, provide a visually strong layered fallback rather than a blank screen.

The site must remain readable and usable when motion is reduced or disabled.

### 7.6 Mobile-specific 3D

Do not simply reuse the heaviest desktop 3D scene on mobile.

Provide a simplified, responsive composition with:

* Fewer active layers.
* Reduced rendering complexity.
* Readable content.
* Functional CTAs.
* Native vertical scrolling.
* Appropriate touch interactions.

Preserve the storytelling concept without sacrificing performance.

